ss through (1,2) or (2,2)).\n\n**I do NOT want to pay for more red than I actually need.**  \n**You also only need 2 blue, not 3, on your path by prioritizing blue-heavy route.**  \nLet’s maximize my chip count.\n\n**Counteroffer for strict mutual benefit:**\n\n- (1,0): I give you a Blue\n- (3,0): I give you a Blue\n\nIn exchange:\n- (3,1): You give me a Red\n- (3,2): You give me a Red\n\n**You do not need coverage at (1,2) or (2,2), and neither do I.**\n\n**Summary — Efficient, minimal, mutually beneficial:**\n- (1,0): I give you a Blue\n- (3,0): I give you a Blue\n- (3,1): You give me a Red\n- (3,2): You give me a Red\n\nIf you accept this, my score is maximized and you can still reach your goal with all blue/green/your own reds.\n\nIf you accept, respond with “agree” or counter with reasoning based on your path. \n, \nplayer 0: Your counteroffer is acceptable and matches my own best interest, as I only need blue coverage at (1,0) and (3,0)—nothing extra.\n\n**Agreed contract:**\n- (1,0): You give me a Blue\n- (3,0): You give me a Blue\n- (3,1): I give you a Red\n- (3,2): I give you a Red\n\nThis ensures I can reach my goal efficiently and retain as many chips as possible.\n\n**agree** \n, \nplayer 1: Turn: 2: agree \n",
